     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks unsafe.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is double the usual maximum for immediate-release metformin, which is 2,550 mg/day (some references say 2,000 mg/day). Even with normal kidney and liver function, a dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n\nHere are some changes you might have meant:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the usual maximum.\n- **1,700 mg/day in total.** For example, 850 mg PO twice daily with meals.\n- **500 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is a common starting dose for a patient who hasn't taken metformin before. It's then titrated up.\n\nPlease check the intended dose, ideally with your resident or attending, and tell me which one to enter. If 1,700 mg three times daily is what the team really wants, please confirm that with the supervising physician. I can then enter it with a note recording that confirmation."
      }
     ],
